      "source": "Anishinaabewaki: the country of the Ojibwe, Odawa and Potawatomi around Lakes Superior, Huron and Michigan, held together by the Council of Three Fires and named here for the largest of the three. Drawn from about 1600, when they were dealing with the French as one, to the treaties of the 1850s that broke the country into reservations. It is a cultural region rather than a polity because the doodemag and the individual bands governed themselves; the council coordinated, it did not rule." }
